r"""AWS benchmark harness for espresso-node: fleet lifecycle, load, and query-service lag.

Runs the shared `netbench` load driver against a Terraform-provisioned fleet in one AWS account
and region, replacing manual `espresso-deploy` runs for this purpose. `plan` renders every file
and prints a cost estimate without writing anything to AWS; `run` additionally provisions,
measures, collects and destroys. `up` provisions a fleet that stays idle for several
measurements: `run --fleet FLEET` measures on it (`fleet.lock` admits one run at a time and a
reset wipes the chain between runs), and `down FLEET` destroys it. The query service's Postgres
runs in a container on node0, on the root volume (`--query-db colocated`) or an extra volume
(`volume`), or on an RDS instance (`rds`); `up --db-modes` provisions the stores, a run picks one.

`status` shows one fleet (phase, instances, agent progress, cost so far) or, with `--all`, every
tagged resource in the region. `destroy --orphans` sweeps runs
whose local state is lost or whose TTL passed. `list` shows the local fleet dirs without AWS
calls; `prune` deletes old ones that hold no AWS resources. `collect` and `render` redo those steps on an
existing run dir. `agent-drive` (on ctl) and `agent-host` (on every host) run under
uv-managed python 3.14 from /opt/bench.

Prerequisites: opentofu and awscli2 (nix devShell), the AWS profile `timeboost-dev` for account
027574771971 (constants below), the default region eu-west-1 (`--region` overrides it), and `--tag`
naming the ghcr tag CI pushed for a `release-*` branch. Each fleet gets a fresh ssh key, deleted
after destroy. `run` pushes its results to the shared results repo unless `--no-publish`;
`publish RUN_DIR...` retries or backfills.
Recipes: `just bench aws plan|up|run|down|status|list|collect|render|destroy|prune|publish`.

Exit codes of `run`: 0 valid, 1 invalid result, 2 refused before any AWS write, 3 failed after
writes but destroyed, 4 resources may remain (the last log line is the destroy command). `run --fleet` never destroys: 3 leaves the fleet up. `up` exits 0 with
the fleet idle, 3 or 4 after a failed provision.

Examples:
    scripts/network-bench/aws-bench plan --tag release-x --nodes 5
    scripts/network-bench/aws-bench up --tag release-x --ttl-min 180
    scripts/network-bench/aws-bench run --fleet --query-db colocated
    scripts/network-bench/aws-bench down <fleet>

FLEET is a name under bench-state/aws, or a path when it contains `/`. `run --fleet`, `down`,
`status` and `collect` without one take the only fleet that may still hold AWS resources.
"""

